WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:06.170
People cannot participate in a decision they cannot discover, understand, or use.

2
00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:14.003
Information equity concerns access to information and also whose knowledge counts when a problem is defined and a solution is chosen.

3
00:00:14.003 --> 00:00:18.773
This episode adapts Kristine Miller's Information Equity chapter under CC BY 4.0.

4
00:00:18.773 --> 00:00:26.664
It condenses and paraphrases Miller's text while excluding all third-party readings, images, quotations, statistics, and linked media.

5
00:00:26.664 --> 00:00:28.613
Information has a delivery chain.

6
00:00:28.613 --> 00:00:36.530
An agency creates it, selects a format, publishes it through channels, and expects a person to find, understand, trust, and act on it.

7
00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:39.247
Failure at any link can exclude participation.

8
00:00:39.247 --> 00:00:44.682
Posting a long technical document online is not equivalent to making its meaning accessible.

9
00:00:44.682 --> 00:00:45.923
Test four dimensions.

10
00:00:45.923 --> 00:00:49.290
Availability asks whether the information exists in time.

11
00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:55.729
Accessibility asks whether language, disability access, device, bandwidth, and format barriers are addressed.

12
00:00:55.729 --> 00:01:00.751
Comprehensibility asks whether a reader can understand the decision and consequences.

13
00:01:00.751 --> 00:01:04.886
Agency asks whether the person can respond before the choice is fixed.

14
00:01:04.886 --> 00:01:07.308
Information also flows toward government.

15
00:01:07.308 --> 00:01:14.456
Residents hold place-based knowledge about timing, routes, indoor conditions, informal networks, and past implementation.

16
00:01:14.456 --> 00:01:20.541
Treating that knowledge as anecdote while treating every dataset as complete creates a false hierarchy.

17
00:01:20.541 --> 00:01:22.135
Create an information audit.

18
00:01:22.135 --> 00:01:28.398
List every audience, channel, format, language, publication date, response method, and responsible staff role.

19
00:01:28.398 --> 00:01:31.188
Test the notice with people who did not draft it.

20
00:01:31.188 --> 00:01:35.572
Provide a plain-language summary without replacing the full technical record.

21
00:01:35.572 --> 00:01:37.565
Preserve disagreement in summaries.

22
00:01:37.565 --> 00:01:39.672
Close the loop with a response table.

23
00:01:39.672 --> 00:01:43.486
For each major comment, state whether the proposal changed and why.

24
00:01:43.486 --> 00:01:46.504
Protect personal information and explain data limits.

25
00:01:46.504 --> 00:01:48.205
Torrance application segment.

26
00:01:48.205 --> 00:01:51.784
This is original local study, separate from Miller's chapter.

27
00:01:51.784 --> 00:01:57.474
The source is the City of Torrance General Plan page, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six.

28
00:01:57.474 --> 00:02:04.807
For a hypothetical Land Use and Safety Element workshop, design an information path from first notice through final decision.

29
00:02:04.807 --> 00:02:13.372
Include a concise project description, map alternatives, accessible files, language access, telephone and in-person options, and a clear deadline.

30
00:02:13.372 --> 00:02:15.250
Explain what is open for change.

31
00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:20.647
After engagement, publish themes, corrections, staff responses, and the next decision point.

32
00:02:20.647 --> 00:02:26.572
Do not claim that this exercise describes the City's actual outreach or determines legal sufficiency.

33
00:02:26.572 --> 00:02:30.442
Information equity turns communication into public infrastructure.

34
00:02:30.442 --> 00:02:33.139
The standard is not whether a file was posted.

35
00:02:33.139 --> 00:02:39.588
The standard is whether affected people had a fair chance to know, understand, contribute, and see the result.

36
00:02:39.588 --> 00:02:47.563
This CC BY 4.0 adaptation credits Kristine Miller, excludes third-party chapter items, and adds a separately cited Torrance application.

37
00:02:47.563 --> 00:02:49.087
No endorsement is implied.
